      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1999273225,
      "version": 1904,
      "versionNonce": 1640342921,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784822783831,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Compute Services : AWS provides us bunch of computation related services, when we talk about compute, we\n    are actually looking out some CPU level resources, we are not looking at databases or storages. We rent\na machine and on that we want to do some process that is a compute service\n\nExample -> EC2(Elastice Compute Cloud) -> using this we can rent scalable virtual machines\nECS(Elastice Container service) -> Using this we can do container management\nAWS Lambda -> Provide us serverless computing engine -> lets say we want to write directly logic on the AWS\n        lambda and we dont want to go with hastle of setting server and managing them.\n\n2. Database Services ->\n RDS(Relational DB service) -> here we can actually opting for any kind of relational db like MYSQL, PgSQL,\n    Amazon Aurora\n DynamoDB -> Amazon own in house NOSQL, which is pritty scalable and lot of use cases\n MongoDB \n\n3. Storage Services -> \n S3(Simple storage service) -> when we want storage for our media or large files.\n EBS(Elastic Block Storage) -> when we want Hardisk or SSd level storage\n EFS(Elastic File System) -> It provides file storage mechanism.\n\n4 Networking Service ->\n VPC(Virtual Private Cloud) -> in order to maintain lot of networking related capabilities.\n Route53 -> to access DNS\n\n5 Security Services ->\n KMS(Key management service)-> we can use this to store our lot of secret keys.\n IAM(Identity access management)-> we can give separate level of access to our developers.\n WAF(web application firewall)-> If we want to maintain some kind of firewall configurations. 26:00",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Compute Services : AWS provides us bunch of computation related services, when we talk about compute, we\n    are actually looking out some CPU level resources, we are not looking at databases or storages. We rent\na machine and on that we want to do some process that is a compute service\n\nExample -> EC2(Elastice Compute Cloud) -> using this we can rent scalable virtual machines\nECS(Elastice Container service) -> Using this we can do container management\nAWS Lambda -> Provide us serverless computing engine -> lets say we want to write directly logic on the AWS\n        lambda and we dont want to go with hastle of setting server and managing them.\n\n2. Database Services ->\n RDS(Relational DB service) -> here we can actually opting for any kind of relational db like MYSQL, PgSQL,\n    Amazon Aurora\n DynamoDB -> Amazon own in house NOSQL, which is pritty scalable and lot of use cases\n MongoDB \n\n3. Storage Services -> \n S3(Simple storage service) -> when we want storage for our media or large files.\n EBS(Elastic Block Storage) -> when we want Hardisk or SSd level storage\n EFS(Elastic File System) -> It provides file storage mechanism.\n\n4 Networking Service ->\n VPC(Virtual Private Cloud) -> in order to maintain lot of networking related capabilities.\n Route53 -> to access DNS\n\n5 Security Services ->\n KMS(Key management service)-> we can use this to store our lot of secret keys.\n IAM(Identity access management)-> we can give separate level of access to our developers.\n WAF(web application firewall)-> If we want to maintain some kind of firewall configurations. 26:00",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
